<!-- Section 1: Overview -->
<section id="overview">
  <h2>1. Dataset Overview &amp; Historical Context</h2>
  <p>This analysis examines every Academy Award winner for Best Original Score across <strong>93 ceremonies</strong>, from the 6th Academy Awards in 1934 through the 98th ceremony in 2026. The dataset encompasses <strong>93 winning films</strong> credited to <strong>79 distinct individual composers</strong> (accounting for multi-composer awards in 8 ceremonies).</p>
  <p>The Best Original Score category has undergone significant evolution since its inception. In its earliest years, the award recognized composers working within the Hollywood studio system&mdash;often European &eacute;migr&eacute;s who brought classical orchestral traditions to American cinema. Over the decades, the category has expanded to recognize synthesized scores, world music influences, and the work of a far more internationally diverse roster of composers.</p>
  <p>Of the 93 ceremonies analyzed, <strong>26 (28.0%)</strong> were won by a composer who had already received the award at least once before, underscoring a pattern of repeated recognition that has persisted across every era of Oscar history.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 2: Top Composers -->
<section id="top-composers">
  <h2>2. Most Awarded Composers</h2>
  <p>Three composers share the all-time record of <strong>4 Best Original Score wins</strong> each: <strong>John Barry</strong> (1966, 1968, 1985, 1990), <strong>John Williams</strong> (1975, 1977, 1982, 1993), and <strong>Ludwig G&ouml;ransson</strong> (2018, 2023, 2024, 2026). Four additional composers have won 3 times each, and nine have won twice.</p>

  <div class="chart-wrap">
    <canvas id="chartTopComposers"></canvas>
  </div>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Rank</th><th>Composer</th><th>Total Wins</th><th>Winning Years</th></tr>
