      <tr><td>8</td><td>Alfred Newman</td><td>2</td><td>1944, 1955</td></tr>
      <tr><td>8</td><td>Nino Rota</td><td>2</td><td>1972, 1974</td></tr>
      <tr><td>8</td><td>Hans Zimmer</td><td>2</td><td>1994, 2021</td></tr>
      <tr><td>8</td><td>Howard Shore</td><td>2</td><td>2001, 2003</td></tr>
      <tr><td>8</td><td>Gustavo Santaolalla</td><td>2</td><td>2005, 2006</td></tr>
      <tr><td>8</td><td>Alexandre Desplat</td><td>2</td><td>2014, 2017</td></tr>
      <tr><td>8</td><td>Trent Reznor</td><td>2</td><td>2010, 2020</td></tr>
      <tr><td>8</td><td>Atticus Ross</td><td>2</td><td>2010, 2020</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Notably, <strong>16 composers</strong> (20.3% of all unique winners) account for <strong>40 of the 93 awards</strong> (43.0%), revealing a pronounced concentration of recognition among a relatively small group of repeat honorees.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 3: Temporal Patterns -->
<section id="temporal-patterns">
  <h2>3. Temporal Patterns &amp; Decade-by-Decade Breakdown</h2>
  <p>The distribution of unique versus repeat winners shifts meaningfully across decades. The 1950s and 1990s stand out as the decades with the highest concentration of repeat winners (40% of ceremonies each), while the 1930s&mdash;with no repeat winners at all&mdash;and the 2010s (just 10% repeats) represent the most diverse winner pools relative to their size.</p>

  <div class="chart-wrap">
    <canvas id="chartDecadeBreakdown"></canvas>
  </div>

  <div class="chart-wrap">
    <canvas id="chartUniquePerDecade"></canvas>
  </div>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Decade</th><th>Ceremonies</th><th>Unique Composers</th><th>New-Winner Ceremonies</th><th>Repeat-Winner Ceremonies</th><th>Repeat Rate</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>1930s</td><td>6</td><td>9</td><td>6</td><td>0</td><td>0.0%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1940s</td><td>10</td><td>12</td><td>8</td><td>2</td><td>20.0%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1950s</td><td>10</td><td>7</td><td>6</td><td>4</td><td>40.0%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1960s</td><td>10</td><td>9</td><td>8</td><td>2</td><td>20.0%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1970s</td><td>10</td><td>9</td><td>8</td><td>2</td><td>20.0%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1980s</td><td>10</td><td>12</td><td>7</td><td>3</td><td>30.0%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1990s</td><td>10</td><td>9</td><td>6</td><td>4</td><td>40.0%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2000s</td><td>10</td><td>8</td><td>8</td><td>2</td><td>20.0%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2010s</td><td>10</td><td>10</td><td>9</td><td>1</td><td>10.0%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2020s</td><td>7</td><td>7</td><td>3</td><td>4</td><td>57.1%</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Key Observations</h3>
  <p><strong>The 1950s: Peak concentration.</strong> With only 7 unique composers across 10 ceremonies, the 1950s had the lowest winner diversity of any full decade. Dimitri Tiomkin alone won 3 times, and 4 of the 10 ceremonies went to repeat winners&mdash;tying the 1990s for the highest repeat rate (40%).</p>
  <p><strong>The 1940s and 1980s: Highest raw diversity.</strong> Both decades featured 12 unique individual composers across 10 ceremonies, the most of any decade. Multi-composer awards in 1941, 1942, and 1987 inflated the individual count, but even controlling for that, both decades saw a wide rotation of winning names.</p>
  <p><strong>The 2010s: An openness era.</strong> Only 1 of the 10 ceremonies in the 2010s was won by a repeat composer (Alexandre Desplat in 2017). The decade introduced 9 first-time winners, including boundary-pushing artists like Trent Reznor &amp; Atticus Ross (electronic/industrial), Hildur Gu&eth;nad&oacute;ttir (drone/minimalism), and Ludwig G&ouml;ransson (African-inspired textures).</p>
  <p><strong>The 2020s: Early signs of re-concentration.</strong> Though only 7 ceremonies have been held, 4 were won by repeat composers. Ludwig G&ouml;ransson alone accounts for 3 wins, and Hans Zimmer returned after a 27-year gap. If this pattern holds, the 2020s could rival the 1950s in concentration.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 4: Era Analysis -->
<section id="era-analysis">
  <h2>4. Era-Based Analysis of Winning Scores</h2>
  <p>Using boundaries derived from the dataset's suggested historical framework, we can characterize four distinct eras of Oscar-winning composition.</p>

  <div class="era-grid">
    <div class="era-card" style="border-left-color: #E74C3C;">
      <h4>Golden Age (1934&ndash;1959)</h4>
      <div class="era-range">26 ceremonies &middot; 25 unique composers</div>
      <div class="era-stat"><strong>Defining figures:</strong> Max Steiner (3 wins), Dimitri Tiomkin (3 wins), Franz Waxman (2), Alfred Newman (2), Mikl&oacute;s R&oacute;zsa (2)</div>
      <div class="era-stat"><strong>Repeat-winner rate:</strong> 23.1% (6 of 26)</div>
      <div class="era-stat"><strong>Signature:</strong> European &eacute;migr&eacute; composers brought late-Romantic orchestral traditions to Hollywood. Scores were lush, leitmotif-driven, and deeply interwoven with narrative. Dominated by studio-contracted composers working at breakneck pace across multiple films per year.</div>
      <div class="era-stat"><strong>Film types:</strong> Sweeping epics (Ben-Hur, Lawrence of Arabia), gothic melodramas (Now, Voyager), and adventure classics (The Adventures of Robin Hood).</div>
    </div>
    <div class="era-card" style="border-left-color: #F39C12;">
      <h4>Silver Age (1960&ndash;1979)</h4>
      <div class="era-range">20 ceremonies &middot; 18 unique composers</div>
      <div class="era-stat"><strong>Defining figures:</strong> John Barry (2 wins), Maurice Jarre (2), Nino Rota (2), John Williams (2)</div>
      <div class="era-stat"><strong>Repeat-winner rate:</strong> 20.0% (4 of 20)</div>
      <div class="era-stat"><strong>Signature:</strong> The studio system dissolved, and new voices emerged&mdash;jazz-inflected scores (Henry Mancini, Burt Bacharach), pop and synth experimentation (Giorgio Moroder, Vangelis), and the rise of the composer-auteur. Orchestral tradition persisted but was increasingly challenged by contemporary idioms.</div>
      <div class="era-stat"><strong>Film types:</strong> War dramas (The Bridge on the River Kwai), counterculture landmarks (The Graduate, Midnight Express), and the birth of the blockbuster with Jaws and Star Wars.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="era-card" style="border-left-color: #2ECC71;">
      <h4>Modern Era (1980&ndash;1999)</h4>
      <div class="era-range">20 ceremonies &middot; 18 unique composers</div>
      <div class="era-stat"><strong>Defining figures:</strong> Alan Menken (3 wins), John Williams (2), John Barry (2)</div>
      <div class="era-stat"><strong>Repeat-winner rate:</strong> 35.0% (7 of 20)</div>
      <div class="era-stat"><strong>Signature:</strong> Synthesizers entered the mainstream (Vangelis, Moroder had paved the way), but orchestral scores dominated the biggest wins. Alan Menken's Disney renaissance dominated the early 1990s. The era saw the highest repeat-winner rate, driven by Menken's three-peat and return wins by Williams, Barry, and Jarre.</div>
      <div class="era-stat"><strong>Film types:</strong> Animated musicals (The Little Mermaid, Beauty and the Beast, Aladdin, The Lion King), sweeping romances (Out of Africa), and prestige dramas (Schindler's List, The English Patient).</div>
    </div>
    <div class="era-card" style="border-left-color: #3498DB;">
