<section id="conclusions">
  <h2>6. Key Takeaways &amp; Forward-Looking Observations</h2>

  <div class="takeaway-grid">
    <div class="takeaway-card">
      <div class="takeaway-num">1</div>
      <div class="takeaway-text"><strong>Composer dominance follows a cyclical pattern.</strong> The 1950s saw concentration around Tiomkin and Steiner; the 1990s around Menken; and the 2020s around G&ouml;ransson. Between these peaks, the 1970s and 2010s represented unusually open fields. If the cycle holds, the late 2020s may see another dispersal of recognition.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="takeaway-card">
      <div class="takeaway-num">2</div>
      <div class="takeaway-text"><strong>Repeat winners account for a disproportionate share of honors.</strong> Just 16 composers (20.3% of all unique winners) hold 43.0% of all awards. The Academy tends to return to composers it has previously validated&mdash;the repeat-winner rate of 28.0% has remained remarkably stable across eras.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="takeaway-card">
      <div class="takeaway-num">3</div>
      <div class="takeaway-text"><strong>The definition of &ldquo;original score&rdquo; has broadened dramatically.</strong> From the lush orchestral tradition of Korngold and Steiner, winning scores now encompass electronic soundscapes (Reznor &amp; Ross), drone-based minimalism (Gu&eth;nad&oacute;ttir), and cross-cultural hybridity (Rahman, G&ouml;ransson). The Academy has consistently rewarded innovation at the boundaries of film composition.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="takeaway-card">
      <div class="takeaway-num">4</div>
      <div class="takeaway-text"><strong>Internationalization is the defining trend of the Contemporary Era.</strong> Of the 22 unique composers winning since 2000, a significant share were born outside the United States, and winning scores increasingly incorporate non-Western musical traditions. This trend is likely to accelerate as global cinema continues to converge with Hollywood.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="takeaway-card">
      <div class="takeaway-num">5</div>
      <div class="takeaway-text"><strong>Ludwig G&ouml;ransson&rsquo;s trajectory may define the next decade.</strong> With 4 wins across 8 years (2018&ndash;2026), G&ouml;ransson is on pace to become the most decorated composer in the category&rsquo;s history. His ability to move between superhero franchises, prestige dramas, and genre films mirrors the Academy&rsquo;s own evolving taste. Whether he breaks the record or the field opens to new voices will be the central storyline heading into the late 2020s.</div>
